<p class="Heading3">Ja 91 The Story about what is Smeared with Posion<br>(Littajātaka)</p>

<p class="summary">In the present the monks are not thoughtful about the use of their requisites, which the Buddha compares to taking poison. The Buddha then tells a story about a gambler in the past who would hide dice in his mouth, until one of the dice was covered with poison, which cured him of his deceit (<a href="../Jataka/091.htm">full story</a>).</p>

<p class="verse3">1. Littaṁ paramena tejasā,<br>
Gilam-akkhaṁ puriso na bujjhati,<br>
Gila re gila pāpadhuttaka,<br>
Pacchā te kaṭukaṁ bhavissatī ti.</p>

<p class="translation">The person, not knowing, swallowed dice smeared with powerful poison, swallow, swallow, wicked gambler, later there will be pain for you.</p>

<p>In this connection, <i>smeared</i> means soiled, stained.</p>

<p><i>With powerful</i> means endowed with supreme poison, with deadly poison.</p>

<p><i>Dice</i> means a small cube.</p>

<p><i>Not understanding</i>: “My swallowing this, will produce such a result,” he does not know.</p>

<p><i>Swallow</i> means you must indeed swallow.</p>

<p><i>Swallow</i>, it is said again to scold him.</p>

<p><i>Later there will be pain for you</i> means having swallowed this dice, later for you there will be acute poisoning, this is the meaning.</p>

<p>&#160;</p>

